<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aheer, Abhira</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ahirs are a community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, traditionally non-elite pastoralists whose occupations were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-herding and agriculture, variously described as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, a clan, a race or a tribe. Their name is believed to derive from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> abhira, though the origins of the ancient Abhira are disputed. From the 1920s many adopted the name Yadav and claimed descent from the mythological king Yadu, part of a movement for higher status in the caste hierarchy under the influence of the Arya Samaj. The British raised four Ahir army companies in 1898 and in the 1920s classed the Ahirs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> as an agricultural tribe, then synonymous with a martial race.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahir.jpg" alt="Ahir" width="330" height="491" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. Forbes Watson and John William Kaye · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_People_of_India_1868_Aheer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Peasant castes tried to rise as groups: under British rule pastoral communities such as the <b>Ahirs</b> took the name Yadav, and tilling castes such as the Kurmis formed caste associations claiming higher status.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
